      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}+7$ for $x\\le -1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=(x+1)^{2}+7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y+1)^{2}+7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y+1)^{2}+7 \\\\ x-7 & =(y+1)^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{x-7} & =y+1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x-7}-1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le -1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le -1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\sqrt{x-7}-1\n$$",
