      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+5$ for $x\\ge 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{2}+2x+5 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2x)+5 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =-((x-1)^{2}-1)+5 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+1+5 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=-(x-1)^{2}+6\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=-(y-1)^{2}+6\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-(y-1)^{2}+6 \\\\ x-6 & =-(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{-x+6} & =y-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{-x+6}+1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{-x+6}+1\n$$",
