      "dom_id": "question-220374",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+5$ for $x\\le 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=4{x}^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{y}^{2}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =4{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{\\frac{x-5}{4}} & =y \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x-5}}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x-5}}{2}\n$$",
